A||||1||Adrift||How tight should I tight the bolts on my rear caliper?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-30-2001||12:41 PM||||I just bought rebuilt rear caliper and am putting the parking brake attachements back on - here's my question - there is a bolt which holds one of hte parking brake pieces on, which tightness affects the piston (The tighter you screw on the bolt, the more the piston moves out of the caliper). How tight should this bolt be? I've screwed it on too tight before, which has resulted in the piston fully coming out of the caliper. Too loose and I'm afraid it will fall off.(Anyone have a torque rating or something?)||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||12-30-2001||02:07 PM||||The only way the piston could posibly come out of the caliper is if the hand brake arm is missing or not seated on the hex portion of the actuator screw. (If be some freak event the arm hole is not hex cut to match the screw then something major is wrong.)<P>The hand brake actuator lever and it's spring are <u>REQUIRED</u> components of the caliper and <u>MUST</u> by installed even if the hand brake cables are not connected to them.<P>Toruque spec on the actuator nut is 35pound feet acording to Haynes...<P>Assembly order for the lever is<P>seal, plastic washer, arm, nut.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
